A major challenge of the larger array assemblies of SKAO, namely AA* and later, is reconstructing images from the enormous amounts of data within given time constraints. This can be tackled through scaleable software pipelines that employ parellelization, typically along the time and frequency axes. We propose a reconstruction framework, within the traditional major-minor cycle image reconstruction paradigm, that allows for decentralized parallelization by another axis, namely, spatial frequency. It achieves this by creating pseudo-full-resolution problems for each node by using the locally stored visibilities together with previous major-cycle reconstructed images from the other nodes. We demonstrate our framework's generality by applying it to both multi-scale CLEAN and sparsity-regularized convex reconstruction, and evaluate its effectiveness by comparing these, in the context of two nodes, against the reconstruction methods' serial counterparts across a variety of simulated and real datasets. We found that the parallelization framework allows for improved reconstruction times for images of similar quality, particularly in the case of our larger datasets where we were able to achieve close to the optimal $2\times$ speedup. These promising results motivate further study of the framework, with the aim of enabling more flexibility when designing scaleable imaging pipelines for SKAO.